<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">A proposed local extension of the χ-field</p>
    <h2 id="t-seam-equation">Where the Dot Would Live Mathematically</h2>
    <p>Let the local integration field vary between the dark scaffold and luminous completion. Define the
      little-red-dot seam as the region where the field is both intermediate and changing rapidly:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \Sigma_{\rm LRD}(t)=
      \left\{x:\frac12&lt;\chi(x,t)&lt;1,
      \quad \frac{D\chi}{Dt}>0,
      \quad |\nabla\chi|>\chi_{\rm c}\right\}.
    \]</p>
    <p>The first condition places the region between the Ledger&rsquo;s middle and final states. The second says
      it is becoming more integrated rather than relaxing back toward the dark state. The third makes it a
      narrow front rather than an entire galaxy glowing uniformly.</p>
    <p>A phenomenological luminosity for the transition could then be written</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      L_{\chi}=\epsilon_{\chi}
      \int_{\Sigma_{\rm LRD}}
      \rho c^2\,\max\!\left(\frac{D\chi}{Dt},0\right)dV,
    \]</p>
    <p>where \(\epsilon_{\chi}\) is the fraction of integration energy reaching radiation. The observed
      spectrum would still be this power plus ordinary accretion light after gas has absorbed, scattered and
      re-emitted it:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      L_{\lambda}^{\rm obs}
      =\mathcal T_{\lambda}(\rho,T,\tau)
      \left[L_{\lambda}^{\rm acc}+L_{\lambda}^{\chi}\right].
    \]</p>
    <p class="lede">These equations make the idea writable, not true.</p>
    <p>Neither \(\epsilon_{\chi}\), the threshold \(\chi_{\rm c}\), nor the evolution law for \(\chi\) has
      been derived from TTI&rsquo;s modified Einstein equations. If they can be tuned separately for every object,
      the proposal explains nothing. The debt is to derive them once and then let the sky refuse the result.
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">Three parts of TTI meet at the dot</p>
    <h2 id="t-three-readings">Integration, Seam, and Torus</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid three">
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>Integration</h3>
      <p>The <a href="/cosmic-ledger/#t-chi">\(\chi\)-field</a> supplies the vertical move from partial to
        complete phase. The dot is bright because a large amount of local geometry is proposed to be crossing
        that interval in a compact time and place. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>Seam</h3>
      <p>The <a href="/seams/#t-horizon">event horizon</a> is TTI&rsquo;s stellar-scale seam: a closure boundary
        that gathers inflow and leaks a transformed residue. The dense luminous cocoon would be the visible
        neighbourhood of that otherwise dark identification. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>Torus</h3>
      <p>Accreting material circulates around an axis while fields and outflows return through a larger loop.
        TTI reads that as its persistent toroidal grammar: the central engine can keep count because matter
        both circles and advances. Ordinary accretion theory already explains the circulation, so the shape
        alone is not evidence for TTI. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

