<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Why early, why compact, why temporary?</p>
    <h2 id="t-timing">The Universe Was Building Its Luminous Layer</h2>
    <p>TTI says dark matter is not inert material waiting beside ordinary matter. It is partial integration:
      the gravitational scaffold that makes complete integration possible. In the first billion years,
      structure formation supplies many dense places where that scaffold is collapsing, baryonic gas is
      arriving and \(\chi\)-gradients are steepening.</p>
    <p>The conjectured order is:</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \underbrace{\chi\simeq\tfrac12}_{\text{dark scaffold}}
      \longrightarrow
      \underbrace{\Sigma_{\rm LRD}}_{\text{compact seam}}
      \longrightarrow
      \underbrace{\chi\simeq1}_{\text{luminous host}}.
    \]</p>
    <p><strong>Early:</strong> many haloes are crossing the threshold at once. <strong>Compact:</strong> the
      transition begins at the deepest closure centre, around the young black hole, before it fills the host.
      <strong>Temporary:</strong> once the outer region reaches the completed phase, luminosity is distributed
      through a galaxy rather than trapped in one red point.</p>
    <p>This also gives a TTI reading of an apparently overmassive black hole in a faint host. The centre has
      completed its seam-work before the surrounding galaxy has completed its luminous body. The black hole
      has not necessarily formed &ldquo;before matter&rdquo;; it has reached the relevant degree of integration before
      most of its host. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">What would turn the picture into an explanation?</p>
    <h2 id="t-predictions">Six Debts the Sky Can Collect</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>One evolution law</h3>
      <p>Derive \(D\chi/Dt\) from the existing action rather than choosing it object by object. The same law
        must produce the onset, duration and disappearance of the phase.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>A source&ndash;host sequence</h3>
      <p>The model predicts central completion before outer completion. Across cosmic time, the compact
        source-to-host ratio should fall in a calculable sequence as the luminous host catches up.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>A measurable χ-gradient</h3>
      <p>Lensing or dynamics should recover a particular radial relation between the non-luminous scaffold,
        the compact transition front and the completed stellar component. &ldquo;There is extra gravity&rdquo; is too
        weak; the profile must be fixed in advance.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card illuminated">
      <h3>An energy ledger</h3>
      <p>Energy attributed to \(L_{\chi}\) must reappear in radiation, motion or curvature without double
        counting the accretion luminosity already supplied by ordinary gravity.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>A discriminating residual</h3>
      <p>After standard black-hole, gas and stellar models fit the spectra, TTI must predict a residual
        correlation they do not: for example a fixed relation among duty cycle, host growth and inferred dark
        scaffold. Without such a residual, TTI is a metaphor laid over a complete astrophysical account.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>A way to lose</h3>
      <p>If no physically stable solution connects \(\chi=\tfrac12\) to \(\chi=1\), if its timescale misses
        the observed population window, or if the required energy exceeds the source budget, this particular
        explanation fails.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--creed">
    <p class="kicker">The TTI reading, without costume</p>
    <h2 id="t-meaning">The Dot Is the Moment the Scaffold Learns to Shine</h2>
    <p>The metaphysical image is simple. Most of the cosmos supplies room and curvature. A smaller share
      gathers without light. At a few early knots, gathering becomes completion so quickly that the boundary
      itself is what we see. The little red dot is not a finished thing but a visible verb: <em>integrating</em>.</p>
    <p><span class="myth">&#9670;</span> That sentence belongs to the philosophy of the framework. The astronomy does
      not require matter to be &ldquo;learning&rdquo;, and no telescope observes completion as such. What a
      telescope can eventually test is the quantitative shell around the image: the \(\chi\)-profile, the
      transition time, the source&ndash;host sequence and the energy ledger.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">Current astronomy in one paragraph</p>
    <h2 id="t-current">The Conventional Account Remains the Benchmark</h2>
    <p>The best-observed little red dots are increasingly consistent with accreting black holes enclosed by
      dense gas that absorbs, scatters and reprocesses their light; some have directly measured central masses,
      and faint stellar hosts are beginning to emerge in deeper or stacked imaging. The population is probably
      not perfectly uniform. None of this requires a \(\chi\)-transition. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p>That is enough current theory for this page. Its purpose is not to reproduce the full astronomical
      debate but to expose what TTI would add, which parts merely rename known physics, and where the addition
      could be tested.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="https://science.nasa.gov/missions/webb/newfound-galaxy-class-may-indicate-early-black-hole-growth-webb-finds/">NASA Webb: the early little-red-dot population and black-hole evidence</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-09900-4">Rusakov et al.: young black holes in dense ionised cocoons</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-026-10579-4">Juodžbalis et al.: a direct black-hole mass in one little red dot</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-026-02945-z">Zhang et al.: extended stellar hosts around stacked little red dots</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--creed">
    <p class="lede">TTI&rsquo;s proposed answer is therefore not &ldquo;the black holes are illusions&rdquo; and not
      &ldquo;standard cosmology is broken&rdquo;. It is that the young black hole and its dense red cocoon are the
      local machinery of a deeper transition: the universe&rsquo;s partially integrated scaffold closing into a
      luminous phase, one compact seam at a time.</p>
